<svelte:head>
	<title>Cycles &amp; Forward References - Macroforge Documentation</title>
	<meta name="description" content="How Macroforge encodes cyclic object graphs and resolves forward references on the way back." />
</svelte:head>

# Cycles & Forward References

`JSON.stringify` throws on a cycle. Macroforge's generated encoders handle cyclic graphs by assigning identities and emitting back-references.

```typescript
/** @derive(Encode, Decode) */
class Node {
  name: string;
  parent?: Node;
  children: Node[];
}

const root = new Node();
const child = new Node();
root.children = [child];
child.parent = root;   // cycle

const json = Node.encode(root, true); // keepMetadata
```

## Metadata

Cycle detection works by tagging objects with `__type` and `__id` as they're visited; a repeat visit emits a reference to the existing id instead of recursing.

Those fields are **stripped from the output by default**. Pass `keepMetadata: true` to retain them: which you must do if you intend to decode a cyclic graph back, since the ids are what reconnect it.

```typescript
Node.encode(root);        // clean payload, cycles flattened
Node.encode(root, true);  // round-trippable, includes __type/__id
```

For API responses you usually want the default. For persistence or transferring an object graph, keep the metadata.

## The context object

`encodeWithContext` / `decodeWithContext` take a context that carries the identity map. The top-level `encode` / `decode` create one for you; you only need the context form when composing encoders by hand.

## Forward references

While decoding, a reference may point at an object that hasn't been constructed yet. Rather than failing, the decoder records a `PendingRef` placeholder and patches it once the target exists.

Two consequences worth knowing:

- The field is temporarily `null` between the placeholder being recorded and patches being applied. If you read the graph mid-decoding you may observe that.
- `ctx.applyPatches()` performs the fix-up. The top-level `decode` calls it for you.

## `freeze`

Pass `freeze: true` to deep-freeze every object produced:

```typescript
const r = Node.decode(input, { freeze: true });
```

Freezing happens after patches are applied, so cyclic graphs freeze correctly rather than being frozen mid-construction.

## Errors

Structural failures inside `decodeWithContext` throw `DecodeError`. The top-level `decode` catches these and returns `{ success: false, errors }` instead: so the entry point never throws, while the internal context form does.
